# The First Star

In the beginning, the world was covered in endless twilight—neither bright nor dark, neither warm nor cold. The people wandered in this gray existence, never knowing true joy or sorrow.

One day, a young girl named Aria grew tired of the monotony. She climbed the highest mountain, determined to pierce the veil above. For seven days and nights she climbed, her fingers bleeding on the rocks, her breath growing thin.

When she reached the summit, she found the sky was not a dome but a vast ocean of sleeping light. Aria reached up and cupped her hands, gathering the luminous water. It burned her palms, but she did not let go.

"Wake up!" she cried, and threw the light as high as she could.

The light exploded into the first star, blazing brilliant and fierce. Its glow spread across the heavens, and where it touched the sleeping ocean, more stars awakened—thousands, then millions.

Aria fell back to earth, her hands forever scarred with light. But when she opened her eyes, she saw shadows for the first time, dancing in the starlight. The people learned that beauty requires sacrifice, and that darkness makes the light more precious.
