Myth: Before time had a name, the world existed in perpetual stillness. Nothing moved—not rivers, not clouds, not even the hearts of the people who stood frozen like statues across the land.

A girl named Lyra alone could move, cursed to wander through this paralyzed world. She touched the frozen faces of her family, pushed against the locked rivers, screamed into the motionless air, but nothing responded.

In despair, she journeyed to the World Tree, whose roots held the earth in place. There she discovered the truth: a great serpent coiled around the tree's heart, squeezing it so tightly that time itself had stopped flowing.

Lyra knew she could not fight the serpent, so instead she sang to it. For seven days she sang lullabies, love songs, and dirges. The serpent, who had never heard music, slowly loosened its grip to listen more closely.

When it finally released the tree's heart, time burst forth like a flood. The rivers rushed, the clouds sailed, and the people gasped their first breaths in eons.

